{:edb/db-before-t 1135 :edb/db-after-t 1136 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534448 50 #inst "2026-10-07T04:39:43.664Z" 13194139534448 true] [17592186166745 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288774 13194139534448 true] [17592186166746 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288781 13194139534448 true] [17592186166746 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288788 13194139534448 true] [17592186166747 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288795 13194139534448 true] [17592186166747 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288802 13194139534448 true] [17592186288774 1017 #edb/ref 17592186288775 13194139534448 true] [17592186288774 1089 "q-118783" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288774 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534448 true] [17592186288774 1100 "Which of the following are second-order ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-x$\n- ${y}^{‴}-y=x-{y}^{″}$\n- ${x}^{3}{y}^{′}+x=y$" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288774 1113 #uuid "d79702dc-a367-5f46-b3e3-98423ca9c490" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288774 1145 "A differential equation is of second-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{″}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″}.$\n\n- Equation II is not a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is not a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{′},$ so this is a first-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288775 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288776 13194139534448 true] [17592186288775 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288777 13194139534448 true] [17592186288775 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288778 13194139534448 true] [17592186288775 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288779 13194139534448 true] [17592186288775 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288780 13194139534448 true] [17592186288775 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534448 true] [17592186288775 1041 "selection" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288775 1104 #edb/ref 17592186288777 13194139534448 true] [17592186288775 1110 #uuid "2e96ac1a-2432-55d3-bc27-2caa9d27225e" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288776 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288776 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534448 true] [17592186288776 1111 #uuid "e0c10430-87d7-52c6-add2-e526283ed255" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288777 1034 "I only" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288777 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534448 true] [17592186288777 1111 #uuid "4e39ae3d-16fa-55b2-ac3c-938c158784ad" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288778 1034 "III only" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288778 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534448 true] [17592186288778 1111 #uuid "7ed16249-f894-5410-81df-30ebb8491557" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288779 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288779 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534448 true] [17592186288779 1111 #uuid "85c840d7-740c-5c4c-b840-0df3adfe1816" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288780 1034 "II only" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288780 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534448 true] [17592186288780 1111 #uuid "a34bb268-45cd-5dd1-bcd9-9034b280af55" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288781 1017 #edb/ref 17592186288782 13194139534448 true] [17592186288781 1089 "q-118858" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288781 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534448 true] [17592186288781 1100 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${e}^{2y}{y}^{′}+1=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+\\sqrt{xy}=2$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288781 1113 #uuid "01f28932-0c22-515d-bae8-91239c9405b7" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288781 1145 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2y}{y}^{′}+1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-{e}^{-2y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-{e}^{-2y}.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{xy} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2-\\sqrt{xy} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=2-\\sqrt{xy}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288782 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288783 13194139534448 true] [17592186288782 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288784 13194139534448 true] [17592186288782 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288785 13194139534448 true] [17592186288782 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288786 13194139534448 true] [17592186288782 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288787 13194139534448 true] [17592186288782 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534448 true] [17592186288782 1041 "selection" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288782 1104 #edb/ref 17592186288784 13194139534448 true] [17592186288782 1110 #uuid "fe564a33-c1d6-5176-82f6-3032a0b8ce4a" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288783 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288783 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534448 true] [17592186288783 1111 #uuid "1e3bcc4d-de1b-576c-8297-c95971769989" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288784 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288784 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534448 true] [17592186288784 1111 #uuid "5f97908e-3f92-5761-ba5b-ac31feead79d" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288785 1034 "III only" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288785 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534448 true] [17592186288785 1111 #uuid "6f1b39b2-5607-56e6-aab0-20d62c41670c" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288786 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288786 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534448 true] [17592186288786 1111 #uuid "df5871ec-2e3b-564e-aa46-5b944ef26a41" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288787 1034 "II only" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288787 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534448 true] [17592186288787 1111 #uuid "f256d90b-8e56-5ec5-acc3-50f179b5ee4c" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288788 1017 #edb/ref 17592186288789 13194139534448 true] [17592186288788 1089 "q-118868" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288788 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534448 true] [17592186288788 1100 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{′}+6x{y}^{2}=8$\n- ${y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x}=3{y}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({y}^{2}+2)$" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288788 1113 #uuid "85f1a1ab-530d-55f2-8d84-69f11e6c2d6e" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288788 1145 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+6x{y}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{8-6x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{8-6x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x} & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =3{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=3{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({y}^{2}+2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288789 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288790 13194139534448 true] [17592186288789 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288791 13194139534448 true] [17592186288789 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288792 13194139534448 true] [17592186288789 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288793 13194139534448 true] [17592186288789 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288794 13194139534448 true] [17592186288789 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534448 true] [17592186288789 1041 "selection" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288789 1104 #edb/ref 17592186288792 13194139534448 true] [17592186288789 1110 #uuid "ec3b71ce-d46a-5ba4-93d2-458731292df6" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288790 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288790 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534448 true] [17592186288790 1111 #uuid "63499214-cd1b-5dc9-b040-f83c23e46068" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288791 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288791 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534448 true] [17592186288791 1111 #uuid "31badfe4-36ce-53b0-8783-9557380e6be1" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288792 1034 "III only" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288792 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534448 true] [17592186288792 1111 #uuid "838926fe-dd34-55af-a20a-56c9e1031bc6" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288793 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288793 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534448 true] [17592186288793 1111 #uuid "ae06d59d-ce7f-56a4-81f3-b75edcc9b47c" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288794 1034 "II only" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288794 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534448 true] [17592186288794 1111 #uuid "9476fec7-72b4-5495-9300-8c79b26682e9" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288795 1017 #edb/ref 17592186288796 13194139534448 true] [17592186288795 1089 "q-96988" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288795 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534448 true] [17592186288795 1100 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $2x-{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\n- $3{y}^{′}-2{e}^{x}y={y}^{2},\\,y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=1$\n- $2x{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{x}={y}^{′},\\,y(2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1$" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288795 1113 #uuid "43085691-e6df-59c5-9601-1a937f0551cc" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288795 1145 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I  is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{y}^{′}-2{e}^{x}y={y}^{2}$ together with the initial condition $y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=1.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2x{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{x}={y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288796 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288797 13194139534448 true] [17592186288796 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288798 13194139534448 true] [17592186288796 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288799 13194139534448 true] [17592186288796 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288800 13194139534448 true] [17592186288796 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288801 13194139534448 true] [17592186288796 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534448 true] [17592186288796 1041 "selection" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288796 1104 #edb/ref 17592186288801 13194139534448 true] [17592186288796 1110 #uuid "0f54ef8c-f6d9-5953-9471-6bb05a0111cb" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288797 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288797 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534448 true] [17592186288797 1111 #uuid "851fb5f7-f80f-53fa-a8aa-a4858b37bea6" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288798 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288798 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534448 true] [17592186288798 1111 #uuid "a5abc364-4dcd-50d2-acf2-dfcce2d06a37" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288799 1034 "I only" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288799 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534448 true] [17592186288799 1111 #uuid "1c1e2821-38b6-54ad-a344-903cbb502127" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288800 1034 "III only" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288800 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534448 true] [17592186288800 1111 #uuid "ddd513d4-0551-51cc-9d9a-ae6737d7be35" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288801 1034 "II only" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288801 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534448 true] [17592186288801 1111 #uuid "b0dea2bc-0164-5177-ab7c-1de240772aff" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288802 1017 #edb/ref 17592186288803 13194139534448 true] [17592186288802 1089 "q-96990" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288802 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534448 true] [17592186288802 1100 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${x}^{3}{y}^{′}-\\sqrt{x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,y(1)=1$\n- $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{‴}-x{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{4},\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2,\\,{y}^{″}(0)=1$\n- $\\sqrt{3}{y}^{′}-3x=2y,\\,y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2)=1$" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288802 1113 #uuid "aba2f021-0a0b-512b-95b0-b907b3e7f2a1" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288802 1145 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${x}^{3}{y}^{′}-\\sqrt{x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=1.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{‴}-x{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{4}$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2,$ and ${y}^{″}(0)=1.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\sqrt{3}{y}^{′}-3x=2y$ together with the initial condition $y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2)=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288803 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288804 13194139534448 true] [17592186288803 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288805 13194139534448 true] [17592186288803 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288806 13194139534448 true] [17592186288803 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288807 13194139534448 true] [17592186288803 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288808 13194139534448 true] [17592186288803 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534448 true] [17592186288803 1041 "selection" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288803 1104 #edb/ref 17592186288805 13194139534448 true] [17592186288803 1110 #uuid "6c728eff-e566-522f-9ebc-4b70900de59a" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288804 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288804 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534448 true] [17592186288804 1111 #uuid "0b783908-d09b-54a0-9131-00d12c35dee1" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288805 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534448 true] [17592186288805 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534448 true] [17592186288805 1111 #uuid "b1e09afb-37fa-588c-9f02-4ea1b3094f22" 13194139534448 t